dogscoff@eudoramail.com wrote:
>I like this a lot, and the same for armour. Corrosion should definitely
>mean something.

Corrosion already means something.  Damaged weapons do less damage than
undamaged weapons (penalty equal to the magnitude of the larger of the two
damage levels), and damaged armour provides less protection (penalty as
per weapons, but if the penalty would be larger than the armour's basic
unenchanted protection value, it gets capped.) The impact of the former,
in particular, is quite pronounced in the early game.

Now, admittedly, it doesn't mean anything if you're running around in a
full set of +5 armours and wielding a +7 artifact, but if you can pull
that off, you can spare a potion of confusion and a few enchant scrolls
with which to damageproof it all.
